{"id":"gcse-electricity-circuits","title":"GCSE Physics: electricity and circuits","subject":"Physics","label":"GCSE","exams":["gcse"],"language":"en","seconds":168,"duration":168.29,"audio":"voice.mp3","words":442,"sections":[{"id":"hook","title":"Everyday puzzle","start":0,"end":15.915},{"id":"explain","title":"Core ideas","start":15.915,"end":50.385},{"id":"explain2","title":"Series and parallel","start":50.385,"end":73.575},{"id":"example","title":"Worked example","start":73.575,"end":99.255},{"id":"check","title":"Quick check","start":99.255,"end":108.735},{"id":"answer","title":"Answer","start":108.735,"end":119.805},{"id":"mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","start":119.805,"end":139.745},{"id":"recap","title":"Recap","start":139.745,"end":168.29}],"lines":[{"s":0,"a":0.5,"b":3.22,"text":"Why does one bulb go out when another keeps glowing?","w":[0.58,0.84,1.12,1.28,1.58,1.84,2.04,2.24,2.56,2.84]},{"s":0,"a":3.67,"b":7.06,"text":"And why does a voltmeter go across a component, not through it?","w":[3.7,3.86,4.08,4.26,4.34,4.86,5.06,5.42,5.48,6.42,6.62,6.88]},{"s":0,"a":7.51,"b":15.49,"text":"I'm Kora. In the next three minutes, you'll learn how charge, potential difference and resistance behave in series and parallel circuits.","w":[7.62,7.8,8.7,8.78,8.9,9.18,9.46,10.1,10.34,10.58,10.8,11.62,12.1,12.52,12.66,13.22,13.62,13.78,14.32,14.44,14.94]},{"s":1,"a":16.34,"b":23.13,"text":"Current is the rate of flow of charge. We measure it in amperes, or amps, using an ammeter connected in series.","w":[16.38,16.84,16.98,17.12,17.38,17.52,17.82,17.94,18.86,19.04,19.34,19.48,19.72,20.22,20.48,21.14,21.36,21.58,21.92,22.4,22.56]},{"s":1,"a":23.43,"b":31.49,"text":"Potential difference, often called voltage, is the energy transferred per unit charge. We measure it in volts with a voltmeter connected in parallel.","w":[23.48,23.96,24.52,24.8,25.08,25.98,26.1,26.28,26.62,27.2,27.48,27.72,28.58,28.76,29.06,29.16,29.28,29.64,29.82,29.88,30.36,30.82,30.94]},{"s":1,"a":31.79,"b":38.14,"text":"Resistance opposes current, and we measure it in ohms. Charge equals current multiplied by time.","w":[31.84,32.44,32.94,33.64,33.76,33.92,34.26,34.44,34.76,35.74,36.24,36.6,37,37.56,37.74]},{"s":1,"a":38.44,"b":42.45,"text":"One volt means one joule of energy transferred per coulomb of charge.","w":[38.62,38.8,39.22,39.72,39.94,40.26,40.38,40.74,41.26,41.46,41.86,41.96]},{"s":1,"a":42.75,"b":49.96,"text":"Potential difference equals current multiplied by resistance. Power equals potential difference multiplied by current, in watts.","w":[42.76,43.22,43.66,44.04,44.42,44.96,45.14,46.36,46.86,47.16,47.58,47.96,48.4,48.56,49.36,49.48]},{"s":2,"a":50.81,"b":58.87,"text":"In a series circuit, the current is the same everywhere. The potential difference is shared between components, and the resistances add together.","w":[50.84,50.96,51.1,51.48,52.26,52.46,52.92,53.04,53.2,53.6,54.6,54.72,55.16,55.58,55.7,56.02,56.32,57.3,57.42,57.54,58.26,58.42]},{"s":2,"a":59.17,"b":66.29,"text":"In a parallel circuit, the potential difference across each branch is the same. The total current is the sum of the branch currents.","w":[59.2,59.32,59.4,59.96,60.72,60.84,61.32,61.74,62.18,62.4,62.82,62.94,63.08,63.88,64.02,64.36,64.74,64.84,65,65.24,65.34,65.46,65.78]},{"s":2,"a":66.59,"b":73.15,"text":"Adding a resistor in parallel decreases the total resistance. That is because there is another path for charge to flow.","w":[66.66,66.94,67.04,67.56,67.7,68.3,68.9,69.02,69.34,70.52,70.72,70.84,71.22,71.42,71.56,71.94,72.26,72.44,72.78,72.9]},{"s":3,"a":74,"b":79.17,"text":"First find total resistance, then current, then the potential difference across one resistor.","w":[74.04,74.34,74.68,75,75.9,76.18,77.06,77.26,77.38,77.84,78.2,78.62,78.76]},{"s":3,"a":79.47,"b":84.47,"text":"A twelve volt battery is connected to two resistors in series, two ohms and four ohms.","w":[79.52,79.64,80,80.26,80.68,80.8,81.22,81.38,81.6,82.16,82.3,83.14,83.48,83.76,83.9,84.18]},{"s":3,"a":84.77,"b":88.78,"text":"The total resistance is six ohms, because two plus four equals six.","w":[84.78,84.92,85.24,85.82,85.96,86.28,86.82,87.16,87.4,87.66,87.98,88.32]},{"s":3,"a":89.08,"b":92.46,"text":"Current equals twelve divided by six, which is two amps.","w":[89.14,89.56,89.94,90.36,90.78,91.02,91.5,91.68,91.82,92.1]},{"s":3,"a":92.76,"b":98.83,"text":"The potential difference across the four ohm resistor equals two multiplied by four, which is eight volts.","w":[92.76,92.86,93.32,93.76,94.14,94.28,94.66,94.82,95.46,95.88,96.18,96.72,96.94,97.76,97.96,98.14,98.34]},{"s":4,"a":99.68,"b":105.35,"text":"A six volt supply drives a current of zero point five amps through a lamp. What is its resistance?","w":[99.74,99.88,100.16,100.44,100.88,101.24,101.32,101.7,101.84,102.24,102.5,102.84,103.06,103.3,103.38,104.26,104.48,104.58,104.72]},{"s":4,"a":105.8,"b":108.31,"text":"Pause here and try it. I'll wait.","w":[105.84,106.18,106.8,106.94,107.26,107.78,107.96]},{"s":5,"a":109.16,"b":114.4,"text":"The resistance is twelve ohms, because resistance equals potential difference divided by current.","w":[109.18,109.32,109.98,110.14,110.66,111,111.34,112.02,112.38,112.96,113.38,113.78,113.94]},{"s":5,"a":114.7,"b":119.38,"text":"Six divided by zero point five is twelve, so the lamp has twelve ohms.","w":[114.82,115.1,115.52,115.7,116.04,116.32,116.62,116.74,117.54,117.74,117.88,118.22,118.5,118.98]},{"s":6,"a":120.23,"b":126.11,"text":"One common mistake is connecting a voltmeter in series. Always connect it in parallel across the component.","w":[120.34,120.52,120.84,121.32,121.44,121.88,121.98,122.54,122.7,123.76,124.1,124.38,124.5,124.6,125.14,125.46,125.56]},{"s":6,"a":126.41,"b":131.99,"text":"Another is saying current is used up. Current is not used up; energy is transferred.","w":[126.5,126.9,127.02,127.4,127.84,128.04,128.3,129.06,129.48,129.62,129.92,130.18,130.8,131.3,131.46]},{"s":6,"a":132.29,"b":139.32,"text":"Do not add resistances in parallel as if they were in series. Also convert milliamps to amps and minutes to seconds before calculating.","w":[132.32,132.5,132.78,132.94,133.58,133.7,134.28,134.42,134.52,134.66,134.82,134.96,135.92,136.24,136.6,137.1,137.36,137.58,137.7,138.02,138.16,138.5,138.8]},{"s":7,"a":140.17,"b":145.13,"text":"Current is charge flow per second, measured in amps with an ammeter in series.","w":[140.2,140.72,140.88,141.24,141.5,141.7,142.52,142.88,143.12,143.5,143.66,143.9,144.44,144.62]},{"s":7,"a":145.43,"b":150.56,"text":"Potential difference is energy per charge, measured in volts with a voltmeter in parallel.","w":[145.48,145.96,146.44,146.7,147.14,147.36,148.28,148.66,148.82,149.24,149.4,149.48,150.04,150.16]},{"s":7,"a":150.86,"b":158.7,"text":"Series circuits share potential difference and add resistances; parallel circuits keep the same potential difference and add currents.","w":[150.98,151.4,151.9,152.18,152.68,153.28,153.54,153.7,154.92,155.42,155.96,156.26,156.44,156.76,157.24,157.76,158.02,158.22]},{"s":7,"a":159,"b":164.12,"text":"Exam tip: write the equation, substitute in standard units, then give the unit.","w":[159.08,159.48,160.22,160.46,160.6,161.5,162.02,162.16,162.58,163.32,163.58,163.76,163.92]},{"s":7,"a":164.42,"b":167.69,"text":"Knowzone is here whenever you want to practise another topic with me.","w":[164.54,165.1,165.22,165.54,165.9,166.02,166.16,166.26,166.68,166.94,167.3,167.48]}],"scenes":[{"at":0.25,"heading":"Everyday puzzle","items":[{"at":0.4,"kind":"text","text":"Why does one bulb go out when another keeps glowing?"},{"at":3.57,"kind":"text","text":"Why does a voltmeter go across a component, not through it?"},{"at":7.41,"kind":"text","text":"Learn charge, potential difference and resistance"}]},{"at":16.09,"heading":"Core ideas","items":[{"at":16.24,"kind":"term","text":"Current","detail":"rate of flow of charge; measured in amperes (A) with ammeter in series"},{"at":23.33,"kind":"term","text":"Potential difference","detail":"energy transferred per unit charge; volts (V), voltmeter in parallel"},{"at":31.69,"kind":"term","text":"Resistance","detail":"opposes current; measured in ohms (Ω)"},{"at":31.69,"kind":"formula","text":"Q = I × t"}]},{"at":38.19,"heading":"Volts and equations","items":[{"at":38.34,"kind":"term","text":"One volt","detail":"one joule of energy per coulomb of charge"},{"at":42.65,"kind":"formula","text":"V = I × R"},{"at":42.65,"kind":"formula","text":"P = V × I"}]},{"at":50.56,"heading":"Series and parallel","items":[{"at":50.71,"kind":"term","text":"Series circuit","detail":"current same everywhere; p.d. shared; resistances add"},{"at":50.71,"kind":"formula","text":"R = R1 + R2"},{"at":50.71,"kind":"image","text":"Left: bulbs in parallel. Right: bulbs in series","src":"circuits.webp","credit":"Brightyellowjeans · CC BY-SA 4.0 · Wikimedia Commons"},{"at":59.07,"kind":"term","text":"Parallel circuit","detail":"p.d. same across branches; total current = sum of branch currents"},{"at":59.07,"kind":"formula","text":"I = I1 + I2"},{"at":66.49,"kind":"text","text":"Parallel resistor: total R decreases; extra path"}]},{"at":73.75,"heading":"Worked example","items":[{"at":73.9,"kind":"text","text":"Find R, then I, then V across one resistor"},{"at":79.37,"kind":"text","text":"12 V battery; series resistors 2 Ω and 4 Ω"},{"at":84.67,"kind":"step","text":"R = 2 + 4 = 6 Ω"},{"at":88.98,"kind":"step","text":"I = 12 ÷ 6 = 2 A"},{"at":92.66,"kind":"step","text":"V = 2 × 4 = 8 V"}]},{"at":99.25,"heading":"Quick check","items":[{"at":99.25,"kind":"question","text":"A 6 V supply drives a current of 0.5 A through a lamp. What is its resistance?"}]},{"at":108.91,"heading":"Answer","items":[{"at":109.06,"kind":"formula","text":"R = V ÷ I"},{"at":109.06,"kind":"text","text":"Resistance = 12 Ω"},{"at":114.6,"kind":"step","text":"6 ÷ 0.5 = 12 Ω"}]},{"at":119.98,"heading":"Common mistakes","items":[{"at":120.13,"kind":"wrong","text":"Voltmeter connected in series"},{"at":126.31,"kind":"wrong","text":"Current is used up"},{"at":132.19,"kind":"wrong","text":"Add parallel resistances as in series"},{"at":132.19,"kind":"text","text":"Convert mA → A and minutes → s before calculating"}]},{"at":139.92,"heading":"Recap","items":[{"at":140.07,"kind":"text","text":"Current: charge flow per second; ammeter in series"},{"at":145.33,"kind":"text","text":"P.d.: energy per charge; voltmeter in parallel"},{"at":150.76,"kind":"text","text":"Series: share p.d., add R; parallel: same p.d., add I"},{"at":158.9,"kind":"tip","text":"Write equation, substitute standard units, then give unit"},{"at":164.32,"kind":"text","text":"Knowzone: practise another topic"}]}],"check":{"at":108.56,"seconds":10,"question":"A 6 V supply drives a current of 0.5 A through a lamp. What is its resistance?","answer":"12 Ω"},"fps":30,"mouth":"","level":"","credits":[{"title":"Series and Parallel Circuit.jpg","author":"Brightyellowjeans","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Series_and_Parallel_Circuit.jpg"}],"voice":"qwen3-tts-12hz-1.7b (cloned voice)"}